  "isLocked": true,
  "description": "Determine optimal unit projection vector maximizing Fisher criterion between two multivariate distributions.\n\nFisher's LDA maximizes the ratio of between-class variance to within-class variance, achieving maximal linear separability.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\n\\mathbf{w} \\propto \\mathbf{S}_W^{-1} (\\boldsymbol{\\mu}_1 - \\boldsymbol{\\mu}_2), \\quad \\mathbf{S}_W = \\sum_{c=1}^2 \\sum_{\\mathbf{x} \\in X_c} (\\mathbf{x} - \\boldsymbol{\\mu}_c)(\\mathbf{x} - \\boldsymbol{\\mu}_c)^T\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Compute class centroid vectors mu1 and mu2\n2. Form within-class scatter matrix S_W summing covariance matrices of both classes\n3. Solve linear system S_W w = (mu1 - mu2), normalize to unit Euclidean length, and round to 4 decimal places\n\n### Returns\nList of floats representing optimal LDA projection direction.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Linear Discriminant Analysis\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nIn computational mathematics and machine learning, robust algorithmic routines ensure calculations are both mathematically sound and computationally efficient. **Linear Discriminant Analysis** implements a core computational building block.\n\nFisher's LDA maximizes the ratio of between-class variance to within-class variance, achieving maximal linear separability. In this problem, the goal is to: determine optimal unit projection vector maximizing fisher criterion between two multivariate distributions.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{w} \\propto \\mathbf{S}_W^{-1} (\\boldsymbol{\\mu}_1 - \\boldsymbol{\\mu}_2), \\quad \\mathbf{S}_W = \\sum_{c=1}^2 \\sum_{\\mathbf{x} \\in X_c} (\\mathbf{x} - \\boldsymbol{\\mu}_c)(\\mathbf{x} - \\boldsymbol{\\mu}_c)^T\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Compute class centroid vectors mu1 and mu2\n2. **Phase 2**: Form within-class scatter matrix S_W summing covariance matrices of both classes\n3. **Phase 3**: Solve linear system S_W w = (mu1 - mu2), normalize to unit Euclidean length, and round to 4 decimal places\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Algorithmic Complexity**: Designing routines with optimal time and space complexity ensures they scale gracefully to large datasets.\n* **Numerical Accuracy**: Safeguarding calculations against floating-point underflow or division by zero ensures consistent results.\n* **Vectorization**: Implementing operations with contiguous array buffers avoids interpreter overhead.",
